<template>
  <div class="rag-story">
    <ProjectPageHeader project-id="pwcRag" context="PwC" data-scroll-reveal>
      <h1>A Structure-Preserving RAG Ingestion Pipeline</h1>
      <p class="project-subtitle">Keeping rules and their conditions together when preparing PDFs for an AI assistant</p>
    </ProjectPageHeader>

    <article class="project-narrative" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">At PwC, I designed and built a knowledge-base ingestion pipeline that prepares complex PDFs for a public-enquiry AI assistant. The assistant uses retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), retrieving passages from published rules and supplying them to a language model to answer a question. My pipeline keeps each rule connected to the conditions and qualifications that determine how it applies, giving the answer the evidence it needs.</p>
      <p>Those relationships could span several pages. A sentence might refer to a later note, a table might continue over a page break, or a flowchart might express a decision through its branches. Extracting the words could still leave the assistant with incomplete evidence if parsing or chunking separated the pieces.</p>
      <p>I placed structural repair before chunking, the step that divides material into searchable passages. This gives the system a chance to recover a rule’s relationships while the surrounding document is still available. The repaired rule and its qualification can then be retrieved together.</p>

      <h2 class="project-chapter-first" id="why-one-pdf-needed-several-ways-of-reading" data-source-anchor="why-one-pdf-needed-several-ways-of-reading">An architecture shaped by the documents</h2>
      <p>The first question was which relationships a PDF parser could preserve and which needed further work. I tested MinerU, a structured PDF parser, against merged cells, a table split across pages, and an interrupted paragraph. It recovered those structures in the test document, giving the pipeline a useful starting point for text and tables.</p>
      <p>A reference such as Note (4) needed a different repair. Recognising the sentence and the note as text still left them several pages apart, where chunking could separate them. Their explicit reference gave me a relationship I could reconstruct and check. I wrote a rule-based linker to locate the matching note, repair splits in its text, and place it beside the sentence that cited it.</p>
      <p>In a flowchart, the words acquire meaning through the arrows and branches between them. OCR could recover the labels while losing the decision path. I compared it with descriptions and Mermaid diagrams produced by a vision-language model (VLM), which can interpret both images and text. Mermaid records nodes, labels, and branches in a text-based diagram format. In the controlled test, it preserved the complete flowchart and supported correct answers to both questions about it.</p>
      <p>These results supported a pipeline in which MinerU handled text and tables, explicit references were repaired by rules, and the VLM reconstructed flowcharts. The repaired material then entered RAGFlow for chunking, embedding, and retrieval. Each method addressed a particular loss of information before that loss could affect an answer.</p>
      <p>I also tested whether one VLM should handle tables as well as flowcharts. The recommendation had to account for the client’s existing infrastructure, operating budget, and maintenance needs, including a preference to operate one AI model where possible. Keeping text and tables with MinerU and using the VLM for flowcharts fitted the evidence from the tests while limiting the work assigned to the generative model.</p>
      <p>A controlled five-page document brought the known failure cases together. I followed the repaired content through chunking, retrieval, and answer generation, using the results alongside infrastructure, cost, and implementation analysis to develop the client recommendation. Open the stages below to inspect the processing steps and their evidence.</p>

